# TECHNICAL SPECIFICATION STANDARD

Feature Specs are the executable technical contract between requirements and implementation.

A Spec should contain enough information for another competent engineer or coding agent to implement the feature without guessing.

## REQUIRED SPEC SECTIONS

# Feature Name

## 1. Objective
## 2. Context
## 3. Scope
## 4. Functional Requirements
## 5. Business Rules
## 6. User Flows
## 7. Data Model
## 8. API / Interfaces
## 9. Backend
## 10. Frontend
## 11. Permissions
## 12. Validation
## 13. Error Handling
## 14. Security
## 15. Performance
## 16. Events / Jobs / Notifications
## 17. Tests
## 18. Acceptance Criteria
## 19. Impact
## 20. Decisions / Open Questions
## 21. Status

Not every section needs content. Use "N/A" only when the section genuinely does not apply.

## SPEC RULES

- Do not describe behavior that the code does not implement unless the Spec is explicitly a target-state specification.
- When implementation changes behavior, update the Spec.
- Avoid implementation details that are not relevant to the feature.
- Record important technical decisions and rejected alternatives.
- Do not leave contradictory rules across multiple Specs.

## STATUS

Use one:
- DRAFT
- READY
- IN_PROGRESS
- IMPLEMENTED
- VERIFIED
- DEPRECATED
